      "message": "Everything altered in this change from this line down feels out of scope for this change. Keeping scopes clearly defined and sticking to them will help us reviewers a lot, and will generally get your change approved quicker.\n\nI suggest getting very familiar with this doc: https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/dev-crafting-changes.html#change-size\n\nOne thing it says is:\n\n\"Do only what the commit message describes. In other words, things which are not strictly related to the commit message shouldn’t be part of a change, even trivial things like externalizing a string somewhere or fixing a typo. This helps keep git blame more useful in the future and it also makes git revert more useful.\"",
